{
  "Version": 2,
  "Name": "Ciosek2000-NCNBPIF-sym-double-spacer",
  "Source": "J. Ciosek, \"Narrow-band interference filters with unconventional spacer layers,\" Appl. Opt. 39, 135-140 (2000). doi:10.1364/AO.39.000135 - Fig. 9 curve 2 (p.139); Eq. (15)",
  "Notes": "Symmetrical double-spacer NCNBPIF, Eq. (15) with m = 3\nSpecification: Double-cavity passband near 652 nm.\nReported: Fig. 9 (approximate): T ~ 1 passband near 645-655 nm, broader and squarer than curve 1, and a second passband near 775 nm.\nNotes: Paper notation: 632.8[(0.5L H 0.5L)^3(0.5H L 0.5H)^6(0.5L H 0.5L)^3] (27 layers after merging). H = quarter wave of n = 2.25, L = quarter wave of n = 1.45 at reference_nm, no dispersion; n_S = n_0 = 1 (Table 1 footnote, Sec. 3), so incident and substrate are both air. Adjacent layers of the same material in the paper's symbol string are merged into one layer (e.g. ...0.5L)(0.5L... becomes one L), which reproduces the paper's own layer counts (27 in Fig. 3, 30 in Fig. 4). Layers are listed in the order the paper writes the symbol string; the paper's Eq. (4) 'S | ... | P' suggests the string starts at the substrate, but because both outer media are n = 1 and the stack is lossless, R and T do not depend on which side faces the light (only the reflection phase does). Normal incidence.\nLayers from Fig. 9 curve 2 (p.139); Eq. (15); layer 1 faces the incident medium.\nNot checked: the paper reports this design only as plots.",
